# [M6.4.3] D27 blinded corpus -- atomic-groups module.
#
# Capture-slot semantics interacting with the cut: a capturing group inside
# an atomic group (or under a possessive quantifier) is retained when its
# own attempt is part of the eventual match, retains its LAST value across
# a quantifier's iterations (even ones that didn't run in the final
# iteration -- the "R22 rules" in docs/spec/match_api.md S5.1), and is
# UNDONE (reported unset) when the branch that set it goes on to fail as a
# whole and a different alternative wins the overall match. All spans and
# group values below are measured against libpcre2 10.46 (see README.md).
#
# Every block carries `features atomic-groups`, per atomic_basic.rxt.

# Basic retention: a capture that ran as part of the winning atomic match
# is reported exactly as it matched.
pattern (?>(a)(b))
features atomic-groups
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2

# "Undone on outer failure": branch 1's atomic body (a)(a)? SUCCEEDS on
# its own first attempt, capturing "aa" (g1, g2) -- but branch 1 then
# needs "bb" and the subject has "cc", so branch 1 fails as a whole and
# the alternation falls through to branch 2, which wins. Branch 1's
# captures, though they were genuinely set during a successful atomic
# sub-match, play no part in the final answer and are reported unset.
pattern (?:(?>(a)(a)?)bb|(a)(a)?cc)
features atomic-groups
m "aacc" 0 4
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 -1 -1
g 3 0 1
g 4 1 2

# Cross-iteration retention (R22 rule 1) through an atomic body: group 1
# ran (and captured) only on the first iteration of the outer *, group 2
# only on the second; both are still reported, not just the last one to
# run.
pattern (?:(?>(a)|(b)))*
features atomic-groups
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2

# Cross-iteration retention under a POSSESSIVE quantifier on a capturing
# group: the group keeps only its LAST iteration's value (there is only
# one group here, so this is R22 rule 1's simplest case, now under ++
# rather than a plain +).
pattern (a)++
features atomic-groups
m "aaa" 0 3
g 1 2 3

# Zero iterations under a possessive star: the group never ran at all,
# so it is unset -- not "empty at position 0".
pattern (a)*+
features atomic-groups
m "" 0 0
g 1 -1 -1

# R22 rule 2 ("an empty final iteration still overwrites") under a
# possessive star: greedy (a?) matches 'a' three times, then a fourth,
# EMPTY, iteration at the end still counts and overwrites group 1 to a
# zero-length span at the end -- exactly as it would under a plain *
# (match_api.md S5.1's own worked example), unaffected by possessification.
pattern (a?)*+
features atomic-groups
m "aaa" 0 3
g 1 3 3

# Nested atomics both retain their own capture.
pattern (?>(?>(a))(b))
features atomic-groups
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2

# The inner atomic's own greed dominates before the OUTER + ever gets a
# second iteration to run: (?>a+) eats every 'a' on iteration 1, so the
# capturing group around it (and the outer +) never sees a second
# iteration at all.
pattern ((?>a+))+
features atomic-groups
m "aaa" 0 3
g 1 0 3

# The empty-iteration overwrite rule firing INSIDE the atomic body
# itself, combined with the outer *'s own empty-iteration stop: (?>(a*))
# grabs "aaa" on iteration 1 (g1 tentatively (0,3)), then matches empty
# on iteration 2 at the end of the run of a's (g1 overwritten to (3,3)),
# at which point the outer * detects an empty iteration and stops.
pattern (?>(a*))*b
features atomic-groups
m "aaab" 0 4
g 1 3 3
